'''Sant Cristòfor''' ({{lang|ca|Sant Cristòfor}}) is the capital and largest city of [[Estret Province]], [[Balboa]], and the principal city of the [[Sant Cristòfor (comarca)|Sant Cristòfor comarca]]. With a population of approximately 5.16 million, it is the [[List of largest cities in Balboa|largest city in Balboa]] and one of the major economic and financial centres of [[Abyala]]. The city is situated on the [[Badia de Sant Cristòfor]], a large U-shaped bay on the [[Shendan Ocean]] coast of the [[Isthmus of Balboa]], and is composed of a peninsula — the [[Península de la Barcelona]] — and the mainland area that wraps around the bay to the east and south.

== Geography ==

Sant Cristòfor occupies a unique geographic position on the [[Isthmus of Balboa]], straddling both sides of the [[Badia de Sant Cristòfor]], a U-shaped embayment of the [[Shendan Ocean]]. The city's territory extends across the [[Península de la Barcelona]] on the western side of the bay and the mainland area to the east and south, covering a total area of approximately 2,550 km². The city's geography is defined by the interplay of the bay, the peninsula, the coastal plain, and the rising hills of the [[Serralada d'Estret]] to the east.

=== The Badia de Sant Cristòfor ===

{{main|Badia de Sant Cristòfor}}

The '''Badia de Sant Cristòfor''' is a U-shaped bay opening to the north into the Shendan Ocean. The bay is approximately 7 km wide at its mouth and extends roughly 10 km southward to its head, covering an area of approximately 50 km². The [[Península de la Barcelona]] forms the western arm of the U, while the mainland forms the eastern and southern arms.

The bay was formed by tectonic subsidence along the [[Sant Cristòfor Graben]], a down-dropped crustal block within the broader [[Balboa Isthmus Fault Complex]]. The graben, which trends roughly north–south, developed as the [[Isthmus of Balboa]] was stretched and thinned by the tectonic forces that created the isthmus itself. The corresponding uplifted block (horst) on the western side of the graben forms the foundation of the Península de la Barcelona. The bay flooded as sea levels rose following the last glacial period, drowning the lower portions of the graben.

The northern part of the bay, near its mouth, is the deepest, with a natural channel exceeding 20 metres in depth that connects to the open ocean. The southern end of the bay is significantly shallower, with extensive mudflats and marshland formed by sediment deposition from the [[Riu Llobregat]] and other streams. This southern shallowing has constrained urban development along the bay's southern shore and has historically made the area important for salt production and, later, industrial land reclamation.

The bay's sheltered waters historically made it a natural harbour, and the [[Port of Sant Cristòfor]] occupied the northern and eastern shores of the bay for centuries. However, the port was relocated to [[Portnou]] in the late twentieth century, and the former docklands have been redeveloped for residential and commercial use. The bay is now primarily used by the [[Ferries de la Badia de Sant Cristòfor|ferry network]], which provides the only direct surface transport link between the peninsula and the mainland.

=== Península de la Barcelona ===

{{main|Península de la Barcelona}}

The '''Península de la Barcelona''' is the western arm of the bay, a low, gently rolling landform that extends roughly 12 km from north to south and 5–6 km from east to west, covering an area of approximately 69 km². The peninsula is relatively flat, with elevations generally below 30 metres above sea level. The peninsula's gentle topography reflects its origin as an uplifted horst block — the tectonic uplift was modest, and the landform has been further smoothed by prolonged weathering and marine erosion.

The eastern coast of the peninsula faces the bay and is the more densely developed side, with the historic [[Pandao]] district, the [[Pandao Ferry Terminal]], and the majority of the peninsula's population concentrated along the bay shore. The western coast faces the open Shendan Ocean and is characterised by a mix of low cliffs and narrow beaches, with some apartment towers along the more accessible stretches. The northern tip of the peninsula, [[El Fort]], is the site of a historic Zong-era coastal battery and marks the western side of the bay's mouth.

=== Shendan Ocean coastline ===

The Shendan Ocean coastline runs continuously from the northern tip of the Península de la Barcelona southward along the peninsula's western shore, around the base of the peninsula, and down through the [[Mare de Déu del Mar (ciutat)|ciutat of Mare de Déu del Mar]]. The character of the coastline shifts markedly along its length.

The northern portion, around El Fort, is the most exposed and rugged, with low rocky cliffs fronting the ocean. Southward along the peninsula's western shore, the cliffs give way to a mix of narrow beaches and developed seafront — the former Platja de Jordi, now lost to coastal development and erosion, was located along this stretch, and the name persists in the surrounding neighbourhood. Apartment towers line the more accessible sections of the coast, though the peninsula's relatively narrow width means the ocean is never far from the bay shore.

At the base of the peninsula, where it connects to the mainland, the coastline transitions into Mare de Déu del Mar. Here the ocean shore becomes wider and more gently sloping, with sandy beaches, private beach clubs, and a series of artificial marinas and yacht harbours cut into the coast. The development along this stretch is lower density — large homes and estates fronting the ocean — with higher density upscale developments set further inland. The marinas of Mare de Déu del Mar are among the most expensive in the city and serve as a focal point for the ciutat's wealthy residential character.

The peninsula is home to the [[Barcelona (ciutat)|ciutat of Barcelona]], with a population of approximately 1.05 million — making it one of the most densely populated areas of the city at 15,143 inhabitants per km².

=== Mainland ===

The mainland portion of Sant Cristòfor wraps around the eastern and southern sides of the bay, forming the larger part of the city's territory. The mainland can be broadly divided into three geographic zones:

* '''The bay shore''' — the eastern coast of the bay, from the northern mouth to the southern head. This is the historic core of the city, home to the [[Original (ciutat)|ciutat of Original]] and the former docklands. The bay shore is densely urbanised, with the city's [[financial district]], government buildings, and main transport hubs concentrated along a narrow coastal strip between the water and the rising ground to the east.

* '''The coastal plain''' — the relatively flat area east of the bay shore, extending 5–10 km inland before the terrain begins to rise toward the Serralada. This plain is the site of most of the city's residential and commercial development, including the ciutats of [[Sant Jordi (ciutat)|Sant Jordi]], [[Sant Martí (ciutat)|Sant Martí]], [[Sant Llorenç (ciutat)|Sant Llorenç]], and [[Ciutat de l'Estació]]. The coastal plain is crossed by several small streams draining from the hills, and the southern portion, near the head of the bay, contains the city's remaining industrial areas and the main railyard.

* '''The [[Serralada d'Estret]] foothills''' — the rising ground to the east and northeast, where the coastal plain meets the Serralada d'Estret. The Serralada ridgelines extend from the northeast (where the range continues into the [[Cumará (comarca)|Cumará comarca]]) southward along the city's eastern edge, enclosing the coastal plain on its north and east sides. There is a relatively flat gap between the hills and the far northern coast of the mainland, which provides a low-lying corridor between the bay and the open ocean shore. The foothills proper begin in [[Sant Miquel (ciutat)|Sant Miquel]] and [[Ciutat del Pujol (ciutat)|Ciutat del Pujol]], which are characterised by lower density, larger properties, and elevated, greener settings. The Serralada itself forms a dramatic backdrop to the city's eastern and northeastern skyline.

To the south, the coastal plain extends flat and unbroken into the neighbouring comarca, with no significant hills to constrain the city's southern expansion. The southern ciutats of [[Sant Llorenç (ciutat)|Sant Llorenç]] and [[Ciutat de l'Estació (ciutat)|Ciutat de l'Estació]] occupy this flat, open terrain.

The northern coast of the mainland, beyond the bay's mouth, faces the Shendan Ocean directly. This coastline — the [[Costa Nord]] — is characterised by sandy beaches and rocky headlands along a relatively flat to lightly rolling coastal strip, before the land rises into the hills of [[Sant Miquel (ciutat)|Sant Miquel]] to the direct east. It has developed as an affluent waterfront area.

=== Ciutats ===

Sant Cristòfor is divided into 11 ''ciutats'' (singular: ''ciutat''; {{lang|ca|city}}), each with its own local council of five councillors elected at-large within the ciutat district. The ciutats are administrative subdivisions with authority over local services, planning, and community matters. Two councillors from each ciutat also serve on the city-wide city council.

