Sant Cristòfor has a [[tropical monsoon climate]] (Köppen ''Am''), typical of the equatorial [[Isthmus of Balboa]]. Temperatures are consistently warm year-round, with average daily highs of 29–32 °C and lows of 22–25 °C, and little seasonal variation. The city experiences a pronounced wet season from May to November, with the heaviest rainfall occurring from June to August, and a relatively drier season from December to April. Annual rainfall varies from approximately 1,760 mm at Sant Martí to 2,248 mm at Barcelona, reflecting the strong influence of the city's geography on local precipitation.

The three weather stations below illustrate the range of conditions across the city. Barcelona, on the peninsula between the bay and the open Shendan Ocean, has the most moderated temperatures and the highest rainfall. Original, on the bay shore with its dense urban heat island, has the warmest nights and slightly less rainfall. Sant Martí, inland and further from the moderating influence of the water, has the hottest days and the coolest nights, with the lowest rainfall.

==== Microclimates ====

The city's complex geography — the bay, the open Shendan Ocean, and the Serralada d'Estret hills — creates several distinct microclimates across the city.

The [[Península de la Barcelona]] sits between two bodies of water, with the sheltered bay on its eastern shore and the open Shendan Ocean on its western shore. The ocean side is breezier and slightly cooler, with consistent onshore winds and salt spray, while the bay side is calmer, more humid, and prone to early morning mist, particularly at the shallow southern end of the bay where warm water and high humidity combine. The peninsula's position between the two bodies of water also gives it a more moderated temperature range than the mainland.

The [[Serralada d'Estret]] foothills receive significantly more rainfall than the coastal plain due to [[orographic lift]] — moist air from the bay and ocean is forced upward by the rising terrain, condensing into heavy rainfall on the windward slopes. The ciutats of [[Sant Miquel (ciutat)|Sant Miquel]] and [[Ciutat del Pujol (ciutat)|Ciutat del Pujol]] can receive up to 30 per cent more annual rainfall than the bay shore. During the wet season, this orographic effect frequently triggers intense afternoon and evening thunderstorms over the eastern ciutats as daytime heating drives moist air upward against the hills.

The coastal plain is vulnerable to [[flash flooding]] during the wet season, when heavy rainfall on the hills rapidly drains through the city's network of rieres into the low-lying urban area. The [[Riu Llobregat]] is also prone to flooding, and its lower reaches have been extensively channelised to mitigate this risk. The flat, open terrain of the southern ciutats — [[Sant Llorenç (ciutat)|Sant Llorenç]] and [[Ciutat de l'Estació (ciutat)|Ciutat de l'Estació]] — is particularly susceptible to standing water after heavy rain.

The [[Shendan Ocean]] coastline — the western shore of the peninsula and the mainland coast through [[Costa Nord (ciutat)|Costa Nord]] and [[Mare de Déu del Mar (ciutat)|Mare de Déu del Mar]] — is exposed to oceanic swell and any tropical weather systems approaching from the north. Storm surge and coastal erosion are recurring concerns along this coastline, particularly at the base of the peninsula and the lower-lying sections of Mare de Déu del Mar.

The dense urban core of [[Original (ciutat)|Original]] generates a significant [[urban heat island]] effect, with temperatures in the city centre regularly exceeding those of the surrounding coastal areas by 2–3 °C. The heat island is most pronounced at night and during the drier season, when the lack of cloud cover allows the urban fabric to retain heat.

== History ==

=== Pre-colonial period ===

{{main|Pre-colonial Balboa}}

The Badia de Sant Cristòfor and its surrounding coastal plain have been inhabited for thousands of years by the [[Birú]] people, the indigenous inhabitants of the [[Isthmus of Balboa]]. The bay's sheltered waters, productive fisheries, and position at the western terminus of the principal trans-isthmian portage route made it one of the most densely populated areas in pre-colonial Balboa. The main Birú settlement was located on the eastern shore of the bay — the site of present-day [[Original (ciutat)|Original]] — where the [[Riu Llobregat]] provided fresh water. A smaller fishing village on the peninsula, on the site of present-day [[Pandao]], was accessible by canoe across the bay. The Birú controlled the western end of the portage route connecting the [[Kendriyan Ocean]] and [[Aethiopian Ocean]] coasts, serving as intermediaries and porters for trans-isthmian trade.

=== Volisanian colonial period ===

{{main|Volisanian Balboa}}

Volisanian explorers reached the bay in 1524, following the Birú portage routes westward. The expedition leader, Cristòfor de Muntaner, named the bay after his patron saint, and the name was applied to the settlement established on the eastern shore. In 1547, the colonial government formally designated Sant Cristòfor as the official Kendriyan port of the isthmian transit corridor, alongside [[Sant Llàtzer]] on the Aethiopian coast, and constructed the [[Camí Ral]] — a paved highway replacing the Birú footpaths — between the two. The Birú population was largely displaced from the eastern shore, and many were pressed into service as porters and labourers.

Sant Cristòfor's position as the Kendriyan terminus of the trans-isthmian corridor made it one of the most important commercial ports in the New World. The city's trade was dominated by flows between the two oceans, but the most lucrative trade was with [[Fosian Lurandia]] — particularly the [[Kingdom of Zong]], which required a reliable route to [[Illypnian]] markets. Zong merchants had traded at the bay since before the Volisanian arrival; the name "Pandao" (from Zong ''bandao'', 半島, "peninsula") attests to this earlier presence.

By the early eighteenth century, the colonial government had progressively increased transit and docking fees on Fosian goods — fees that were explicitly discriminatory. The [[First War of the Isthmus]] (1711–1716) resulted when Zong launched a military expedition to seize the city. The [[Treaty of Remoria]] (1716) granted Zong docking and passage rights, and in 1723 the city was designated a [[free port]] — goods could be imported, stored, and re-exported without standard colonial transit duties. The free port status transformed the city into a cosmopolitan entrepôt attracting Volisanian, Zong, and [[Nakamizu]] merchants.

By the mid-eighteenth century, Sant Cristòfor had become the principal hub of the [[trans-Kaftian slave trade]] in Balboa. Enslaved [[Kaftians]] were brought ashore at the city's wharves and sold at auction for transport to the plantations of the Estret and Migjorn coastal plains. The slave trade was abolished in 1807, and slavery itself in 1848 — making Balboa the second-to-last Volisanian colony to abolish it. The city's distinctive urban culture — the syncretic [[istmo son]] musical tradition, the emergence of [[Balboan Creole]], and the mixing of Illypnian, Fosian, Kaftian, and indigenous influences — developed during this cosmopolitan colonial period.

=== First Republic (1854–1899) ===

{{main|First Republic of Balboa}}

With independence in 1854, Sant Cristòfor became the capital of the [[Republic of Balboa]]. The colonial government buildings on the bay shore were repurposed as the seat of the new republic. The city's municipal government was dominated by a succession of deeply corrupt mayors who served as brokers between commercial interests and the national government. The most significant construction project was the [[Grand Train Terminal|Estació Gran de Sant Cristòfor]] (1868–1872), the western terminus of the [[Isthmus Railway]], whose construction was mired in the [[Escàndol Ferroviari]] (1866). Mayor [[Joan Ballester i Forteza]] was assassinated on the steps of the terminal in 1886 by a disgruntled former municipal employee.

The city grew from approximately 80,000 at independence to over 200,000 by the 1880s. In 1866, the city was formally divided into six districts reflecting its racial geography: the Coast (commercial waterfront), the Market (central commercial), the Northern District (white elite), the Eastern District (Zong community), the Southeastern District (mixed-race), and the Southern District (Black working-class). The city annexed [[Dàrsena]] in 1872 to expand warehouse and dock facilities, and [[Costa Nord]] in 1880 to prevent wealthy residents from leaving the tax base. A network of canals was cut in the southern districts during the late 1800s, allowing boats direct access to warehouses.

The construction of the [[Balboa Canal]] (1870s–1885) was a mixed blessing: the canal's terminus was placed at [[Portnou]] rather than Sant Cristòfor, and the city lost its role as the primary transshipment point. President [[Rodrigo Velásquez]]'s anti-Fosian policies escalated discrimination against the Zong community, contributing to the [[Second War of the Isthmus]] (1893–1899). Zong forces occupied the city in 1893; the Black and mixed-race populations, who had little stake in the republic, continued daily life under occupation with minimal disruption. The war ended with the [[Treaty of Sant Llàtzer]] (1899), transferring Sant Cristòfor to Zong.

=== Zong Balboa period (1899–1950) ===

{{main|Zong Balboa}}

The Zong administration abolished the elected mayoralty and appointed a [[Grand Administrator]] (總行政官) to govern the city. The city's territory was dramatically expanded to encompass the modern comarcas of Sant Cristòfor, [[Cumará]], and parts of [[Baix Llevant]] and [[Els Aiguamolls]]. The old city was divided into three ciutats (Sant Cristòfor Central, Dàrsena, Costa Nord), and the peninsula — previously a separate community — was incorporated and split into Barcelona and Pandao.

The Zong administration invested heavily in public works: a unified water and sewer system, paved roads, street lighting, and a unified police and fire service. Governor [[Lîm Bûn-cheng]] (1910–1925) introduced universal suffrage and anti-discrimination protections between 1912 and 1918, partly as genuine reform and partly to attract Black Balboan workers from the rump state. The period from 1910 to 1935 became known as the [[Shining Star era]] — a time of rapid economic growth, cultural flourishing, and relative egalitarianism. The territory's population grew from 312,000 in 1900 to 2.55 million by 1934. A multilingual press (''[[Seng-kì-pò]]'', ''[[El Faro del Istmo]]'', ''[[Chương Châu Nhật Báo]]'', ''[[New Horizon]]''), the [[Sant Cristòfor School of painting]], and the [[Sant Cristòfor Municipal Theatre]] (1921) characterised the era's cultural life.

The Shining Star's egalitarianism was more legal than social. The city functioned as two settlements: a prosperous Zong-dominated core with modern infrastructure, and a periphery of informal exurbs housing the Sinchewese and Black working populations. [[Pandao]], on the peninsula, became the home of the emerging Black middle class — merchants, doctors, educators who settled there for its space, distance from overcrowding, and insulation from disease. The first two [[Metro de Sant Cristòfor|Metro]] lines were built during this period, both originating underneath the Grand Train Terminal and running eastward to mainland neighbourhoods. Three separate private streetcar networks — the [[Sant Cristòfor Tramway Company]], the [[Costa Nord Light Railway]], and the [[Pandao Tram Company]] — operated with no unified fare system. A planned Line 3 (north–south connector) was halted by the outbreak of war.

The [[Great Adonian War]] (1935–1947) devastated the city. The siege persisted for the duration, causing widespread famine, disease, and displacement among the predominantly Black population. The Metro tunnels were used as air-raid shelters. The Zong population evacuated disproportionately; the Zong share of the population collapsed from 15% to 2%. The territory that emerged from the siege was overwhelmingly Black and mixed Balboan.

=== AC mandate period (1950–1960) ===

{{main|International Mandate of Sant Cristòfor}}

The [[Adonian Community]] mandate restored elected government with a mayor and city council. Mayor [[Elena Vargas i Aldana]] (1951–1956) oversaw the rebuild strategy: the restoration of the [[Grand Train Terminal]] (completed 1953), the creation of the boulevard system and "mini city" concept in Sant Cristòfor Central, and the merger of [[Pandao]] and Barcelona into a single ciutat. Mayor [[Rafael Crespo i Ousmane]] (1956–1960) oversaw construction of the [[Eastern Highway]] — an elevated expressway that became the most criticised element of the rebuild — and the prohibition of a coastal highway along the Shendan Ocean coast, a ban that remains in force. The [[Second Star]] — the mandate period's cultural and economic flowering — produced a second wave of skyscraper construction by the emerging Black capitalist class and the [[No Return]] movement, whose mass demonstrations forced the AC to impose the [[Twelve Guarantees]] as a condition of reunification. The city's airport was rebuilt at [[Ciutat de l'Estació]] in 1954.

=== Post-mandate period and reunification (1960–1973) ===

The mandate territory was returned to Balboa in 1960 under the Twelve Guarantees. The [[Balboan People's Party]] (BPP) won the first post-Guarantees elections. Between 1961 and 1963, the Balboan government carried out a territorial reorganisation that broke up the vast Zong-era district into separate comarcas, reducing Sant Cristòfor to its modern boundaries. The city's current ciutat structure — 11 ciutats, each with its own local council — was formally established. The historic core was renamed [[Original (ciutat)|Original]] ("the original Sant Cristòfor") for the first time, replacing the Zong-era name "Sant Cristòfor Central."

The Metro was expanded with three new lines: Line 3 (north–south, 1964), Line 4 (east to suburbs, 1968), and Line 5 (around the northern bay shore to the peninsula, 1971). In 1964, after the Costa Nord Light Railway became insolvent, the three private streetcar companies were merged into the Metro system under the [[Autoritat de Transport de Sant Cristòfor]] (ATS), which introduced a unified fare system. Heavy industry expanded in Dàrsena — steel mills, chemical plants, and manufacturing — bringing jobs but also severe pollution. The population of Original declined steadily as families moved to the outer ciutats, and the old Southern District was hollowed out. Full reunification was achieved with the [[Treaty of Areza]] (1973), which returned the canal to Balboan sovereignty.

=== Post-reunification era (1973–2000) ===

The Treaty of Areza coincided with a sustained suburban boom. The outer ciutats grew rapidly as families moved out of Original and Dàrsena. The new [[Sant Cristòfor International Airport]] opened in 1985, connected by an extension of Metro Line 1 (1986). The old Ciutat de l'Estació airport was redesignated [[Areza International Airport]], serving the [[International City of Areza]] — a compromise the Balboan government accepted reluctantly, wanting the prime industrial land for development. The AC funded Metro Line 9 (Sant Martí to Areza via the canal corridor), completed in 2003, which allowed Areza International to close.

Dàrsena was progressively deindustrialised: the steel mill closed in 1982, the chemical plant in 1987, and the last major facilities in the early 1990s, as industry relocated to the canal zone. The empty warehouses were converted into loft apartments, artists' studios, and restaurants from the early 1990s onward — a celebrated urban regeneration that also displaced the working-class community through gentrification. Original was slowly repopulated by young professionals in the 1990s, transforming the demographic profile from Black working-class to increasingly white and affluent.

=== Urban renaissance (2000–2015) ===

The turn of the millennium marked a period of rapid growth driven by finance and services. A new generation of glass-and-steel towers joined the Second Star skyscrapers. Dàrsena became the city's nightlife centre — bars, clubs, and live music venues producing a distinctive Sant Cristòfor sound blending [[istmo son]] with electronic, Zong, and Kaftian influences. The city government responded to nightlife complaints with progressively stricter regulations on closing times, noise, and licensing, squeezing the creative community that had driven the renaissance.

Municipal politics alternated between the BPP and the [[Liberal Party of Balboa|Liberal Party]] from 2000 onward. On 12 March 2003, a bomb detonated underneath the [[National Assembly of Balboa|National Assembly building]] in Original, where it sat above the Metro Line 5 tunnel. The bomber, [[Adrià Vallmajor i Soler]], was a lone white nationalist acting over social grievances; 31 people were killed. The damaged section of Line 5 was closed for eleven months, and permanent security measures were introduced across the Metro system. Under the BPP administration from 2005, construction on Lines 6, 7, and 8 was restarted; Line 6 (southern bay shore to Barcelona) was completed in 2008, Line 7 (Sant Miquel and Ciutat del Pujol) in 2012, and Line 8 (Dàrsena through Sant Llorenç) in 2015, giving the city a nine-line Metro system.

=== Recent period (2015–present) ===

In 2015, the city launched a street safety programme — lower speed limits, redesigned intersections, and the creation of a cycling network along Original's boulevards and avenues. On 17 October 2016, a fire in a converted Dàrsena warehouse killed 12 people, mostly young artists in unlicensed spaces, exposing dangerous conditions in the warehouse conversions and provoking mass protests — the largest since the No Return demonstrations. The [[Adonian pandemic]] of 2020 shut the city down, devastating the service economy and accelerating remote-work trends that left office vacancy rates above 30% by 2022.

The city held three referendums (2004, 2008, 2016) on extending Metro Line 1 under the bay to Barcelona; all passed, but the extension was blocked by the [[Maritime Workers Party]] (PTM), representing dockworkers who would lose their ferry jobs. In 2025, the BPP mayor [[Núria Gomila i Renom]] was arrested in a financial crimes investigation, triggering a snap election won by [[Oriol Cardona i Ousmane]] of the [[Socialist Front of Balboa]] (FSB) — the first non-BPP, non-Liberal mayor since the post-Guarantees era. Cardona i Ousmane pledged to build the Line 1 extension with dockworker retraining, expand social housing, and strengthen tenants' rights.

== Government ==

{{main|Mayors of Sant Cristòfor}}

Sant Cristòfor is governed by an elected [[mayor]] and a [[city council]]. The mayor serves four-year terms with a two-term limit (8 years maximum), and has executive authority over municipal administration, planning, and major infrastructure. The current mayor is [[Oriol Cardona i Ousmane]] ([[Socialist Front of Balboa|FSB]]), elected in 2025.

The city council is composed of 22 councillors — two elected at-large from each of the city's 11 ciutats. The council has authority over local services, planning, and community matters within each ciutat, while the mayor and city council retain authority over cross-ciutat issues including transport, major infrastructure, and economic development. Each ciutat also elects its own local council of five councillors, elected at-large within the ciutat district, with authority over local services and planning.

The city is the seat of the [[National Assembly of Balboa]], housed in the National Assembly building in [[Original (ciutat)|Original]].

== Economy ==

{{main|Economy of Balboa}}

Sant Cristòfor is the principal economic centre of [[Balboa]] and one of the major financial hubs in [[Abyala]]. The city's metropolitan area accounts for approximately $200 billion of [[Estret Province]]'s $224 billion GDP — roughly 89 per cent of the provincial economy and 63 per cent of national GDP. GDP per capita in the metropolitan area is approximately $38,800, more than four times the national average of $9,100 and the highest of any metropolitan area in Balboa. The city's economy is dominated by services, which account for approximately 80 per cent of metropolitan GDP; industry contributes 17 per cent, and agriculture less than 3 per cent.

The city's economic role derives from its position at the junction of the [[Illypnian]], [[Fosian]], and [[Kaftian]] markets; its proximity to the [[Balboa Canal]] and the [[International City of Areza|AC institutions at Areza]]; and its favourable regulatory environment, including low taxation, minimal capital controls, and strong rule of law under the [[Twelve Guarantees]] framework.

=== Financial services ===

{{main|Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor}}

Financial services are the largest sector of the Sant Cristòfor economy, accounting for approximately 30 per cent of metropolitan GDP. The city is home to the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]], the largest stock exchange in the isthmus region, and hosts regional headquarters of major international banks, insurance companies, and investment management firms. Banking, insurance, and investment management collectively account for approximately 18 per cent of national GDP, the vast majority of which is generated in Sant Cristòfor.

The city's financial district is concentrated along the [[Original (ciutat)|Original]] bay shore, where the major banks, the Bolsa, and the headquarters of the [[Balboa Canal Authority]] and the [[Balboan Investment Authority]] are located. The favourable regulatory environment — low corporate tax rates, minimal capital controls, and strong legal protections under the Guarantees — has made Sant Cristòfor a regional centre for international banking, offshore fund management, and insurance. The city's role as a financial hub is reinforced by the presence of the AC institutions in nearby [[International City of Areza|Areza]], which provide a stable institutional framework and a source of demand for legal, accounting, and advisory services.

=== Canal and commercial services ===

While the major port facilities and the [[Sant Cristòfor Free Trade Zone]] are located in neighbouring [[Portnou]], Sant Cristòfor serves as the commercial and professional services hub for the canal economy. Shipping agencies, freight forwarders, customs brokers, and marine insurance firms are concentrated in the [[Original (ciutat)|Original]] bay shore area, providing the legal, financial, and advisory services that support the canal's operation. The [[Balboan Ship Registry]], one of the world's principal flags of convenience, is administered from Sant Cristòfor. The city's proximity to the canal — and to the [[International City of Areza|AC institutions at Areza]] — makes it the natural base for the professional and financial services that the canal economy generates, even though the physical logistics operations are centred elsewhere.

=== Tourism ===

{{main|Tourism in Balboa}}

Tourism is a significant and growing sector, contributing approximately 8 per cent of metropolitan GDP. The city received approximately 1.8 million cruise passengers in 2025, making it the busiest cruise port in Balboa. The principal attractions include the historic centre of [[Original (ciutat)|Original]] — a [[World Heritage Site]] — the [[Badia de Sant Cristòfor]] waterfront, the nightlife district of [[Dàrsena (ciutat)|Dàrsena]], and the beaches of [[Costa Nord (ciutat)|Costa Nord]] and [[Mare de Déu del Mar (ciutat)|Mare de Déu del Mar]]. The city's cultural life — the [[istmo son]] musical tradition, the [[Sant Cristòfor School of painting]], and the multilingual heritage of the colonial and Zong periods — is a significant draw for visitors from Illypnia and Fosia.

The hospitality sector is concentrated in Original (business and heritage hotels), Barcelona (resort and conference hotels), and Dàrsena (boutique hotels and short-term rentals in converted warehouses). The cruise terminal, located on the Original bay shore, handles year-round arrivals from Illypnian, Fosian, and Kaftian ports.

=== Light manufacturing ===

Light manufacturing accounts for approximately 10 per cent of metropolitan GDP and is concentrated in the [[Sant Cristòfor Free Trade Zone]] and the industrial areas of [[Sant Llorenç (ciutat)|Sant Llorenç]] and [[Ciutat de l'Estació (ciutat)|Ciutat de l'Estació]]. The sector is oriented toward assembly for re-export, using imported components to produce consumer goods, textiles, and electronics for the Abyalan market. The Free Trade Zone's tax incentives and streamlined customs procedures have attracted manufacturers from Zong, Louyang, and Volisania, though the sector remains vulnerable to competition from lower-cost producers in other Abyalan countries.

The former heavy industrial base of [[Dàrsena (ciutat)|Dàrsena]] — steel mills, chemical plants, and manufacturing — was progressively deindustrialised from the 1980s onward as industry relocated to the canal zone. The remaining industrial activity in Dàrsena consists of small-scale food processing and artisanal workshops.

=== Technology and startups ===

An emerging technology sector has developed in [[Dàrsena (ciutat)|Dàrsena]] since the late 2010s, capitalising on foreign investment and the city's position as a gateway to the Abyalan market. Municipal and provincial programmes, launched between 2017 and 2020, offer tax incentives, subsidised office space in converted warehouses, and access to venture capital funds targeting Abyalan-focused startups. The majority of startups in the district serve the Abyalan market — [[fintech]], logistics technology, [[agritech]], and e-commerce platforms — rather than competing in global markets. The sector remains small relative to financial services and logistics, but has attracted attention from Zongese and Illypnian venture capital firms seeking exposure to the Abyalan consumer market.

=== Media and entertainment ===

[[Costa Nord (ciutat)|Costa Nord]] is the centre of Balboa's music, film, and entertainment industry. The ciutat's recording studios, production houses, and post-production facilities serve the domestic market and the wider Abyalan entertainment sector. The [[istmo son]] musical tradition, which originated in Sant Cristòfor's Black and mixed communities, remains the foundation of the city's music industry, though contemporary producers increasingly blend it with electronic, Zong, and Kaftian influences. The Balboan film industry, while smaller than those of Zong or Louyang, produces a growing number of features and series for the Abyalan market, with Costa Nord's lower production costs and multilingual talent pool providing a competitive advantage.

=== Employment and income ===

The Sant Cristòfor metropolitan labour force is approximately 2.8 million (2026 estimate). Unemployment in the metropolitan area is approximately 5.2 per cent — significantly below the national average of 7.4 per cent — reflecting the concentration of high-value services in the city. However, unemployment rates vary sharply across the ciutats: Original and Barcelona have rates below 4 per cent, while Sant Llorenç and Ciutat de l'Estació have rates above 8 per cent.

Median household income in the metropolitan area is approximately $22,400 — more than three times the national median of $6,700 — but income inequality within the city is significant. The Gini coefficient for the metropolitan area is approximately 46, driven by the contrast between the high-income professional class in Original, Barcelona, and Costa Nord, and the lower-income service and industrial workforce in Sant Llorenç, Ciutat de l'Estació, and Sant Miquel. The cost of living in Sant Cristòfor — particularly housing — is the highest in Balboa, and the national minimum wage of approximately $420 per month is widely regarded as insufficient to meet basic needs in the city.

== Sport ==

=== Football ===

{{main|Balboa football league system}}

Sant Cristòfor is the centre of [[Balboa]]n [[association football|football]], with five clubs in [[La Lliga de l'Istme]] — the top tier of the [[Balboa football league system]]. The city's clubs reflect its diverse communities: the port and commercial class, the Black Balboan community, the Zong community, the Sinchewese community, and the northern suburbs.

